# itr-prep credentials -- copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; keep it that way.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# Nothing in here is ever printed, logged, put in an error message, or passed on a command
# line. `itr-prep unlock` reads these values itself and reports only which VARIABLE opened a
# file. If you are being helped by an AI agent, it should run `itr-prep unlock --input <path>`
# and never see, ask for, or handle the password. That is the whole reason this file exists
# instead of the password living in a filename.
#
# Check what the tool can see, without revealing anything:
#
#   itr-prep unlock --list-credentials
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Derived passwords -- set these two and most documents need nothing else
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Form 16 from most payroll providers, and several depository and CAS statements, are not
# protected by an arbitrary secret but by a formula: the PAN in lower case followed by the
# date of birth as DDMMYYYY. Declare both once and the candidates are derived, so you do
# not have to enumerate a password per file.
#
# PAN in the usual five letters, four digits, one letter. Case does not matter here; both
# cases are tried.
ITRPREP_PAN=AAAAA9999A

# Date of birth as DDMMYYYY -- eight digits, no separators. 2 March 1985 is 02031985.
# A wrong format here is the most common reason an unlock fails.
ITRPREP_DOB=01011990

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Literal passwords -- one variable per document class
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Convention: ITRPREP_PW_<LABEL>, where <LABEL> names the source in capitals. The label is
# for you; the tool tries EVERY ITRPREP_PW_* variable against every file, so a file does not
# need to be named after its password and never should be.
#
# Add or remove these freely -- any variable starting with ITRPREP_PW_ is picked up.
#
# ITRPREP_PW_FORM16=
# ITRPREP_PW_ETRADE=
# ITRPREP_PW_FIDELITY=
# ITRPREP_PW_SCHWAB=
# ITRPREP_PW_COMPUTERSHARE=
# ITRPREP_PW_NSDL_CAS=
# ITRPREP_PW_CDSL_CAS=
# ITRPREP_PW_AIS=
# ITRPREP_PW_26AS=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where decrypted copies go
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default work/unlocked, created owner-only (0700) with each file written 0600. It sits
# under work/, which is already gitignored. Delete the whole directory when you are done:
#
#   rm -rf work/unlocked
#
# ITRPREP_UNLOCK_DIR=work/unlocked
